// One grammar rendered twice, which is what a package ships and what the stand measures.
//
// The expression language ships `ExpressionParser` and `ExpressionParser.Immediate`:
// the same grammar compiled again with a suffix of its own and a carrier that builds
// where it reads. Every paired run on the stand reads both, and until this snapshot
// existed neither the suffixed naming nor the immediate carrier appeared in any file a
// change is reviewed in — the carrier is chosen per compilation, not per grammar, so no
// grammar could bring it here.
//
// Two files stand beside this one: Twice.gram.g.cs, carried on the tape by `Auto`, and
// Twice.Immediate.gram.g.cs, which builds each value as its rule accepts. They are the
// same language and the same answers; the diff between them is the carrier itself.
//
// A guard is what makes the two differ where it matters: a value a guard names has to
// exist while the text is still being read, which the tape builds from the log and the
// immediate carrier already has in a register.

trivia = { ' '* }

Sum : @int = first: Term & rest: More* & when @(first >= 0) => @(first + rest.Length)

More : @int = '+' & value: Term => @(value)

Term : @int
	= '(' & inner: Sum & ')' => @(inner)
	| digits: Digits         => @(digits)

Digits : @int = text: ['0'..'9']+ => @(int.Parse(text))

parse Sum
